{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on exact design candidate cfb8d0fe0db769a3404e7685fbbb3271596feaab. All three exact-head artifacts inspected: notes/2026-10-02-canonical-mint-ownership.md, docs/protocol.md and plans/README.md. No production source differs from base 3ac55e96.\n\nArchitecture: Room storage/alarm recovery, Git mint and landing ownership, Publisher/log credentials and check-job credentials are affected. The design answers the four findings in git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:9ff903ab45c9a7a69fd46250a9c76891f77bd832: a durable wake before every send, maintained takeover wake during sent/held states, IDs classified separately from plaintext and late request answers, and one shared observation with indexed batches and paged duties. Revision 3 also supplies an explicit artroom_land_token primary-key projection, updated transactionally by pushToken/tokenRevoked, so inventory observations need not parse lifetime push histories. It explicitly retains jobs.issue's absolute deadline checks. Those are valuable complete-scope repairs. Same-head section 31 carries the intended normative change, but its remaining interface and lifecycle contradictions below must be resolved before approving this design. There is no docs/reference/architecture.md in this repository.\n\nSecurity: unknown effects remain honest open duties; no canonical sweep or attribution by timestamps is authorized. Provider evidence now separates the Workers binding, REST documentation and local assumptions, including REST created_at and the undocumented apply-time/closure fence. Owner transfers remain conditional and atomic. This is an unimplemented design: approval would authorize neither live operations nor lanes A-C implementation; each still requires its own adopted provenance and request. Existing job guards must remain expressible through the ledger API.\n\nSimplification: a common ledger, one observation summary and token-ID projections remove repeated observations and hidden non-idempotent retries. Retaining unknown records is necessary; bounded access is the way to scale them. The remaining work is to make the proposed contract internally consistent rather than add another subsystem.\n\n1. P2: the stated mint result cannot support the absolute deadline check. notes/2026-10-02-canonical-mint-ownership.md:277 returns only {id, plaintext, release(), claim()}, while lane C now requires inspecting the provider's reported expiry against the job's deadline. Current packages/room/src/jobs.ts@cfb8d0fe:383-386 reads t.expiresAt; arrival-plus-requested-lifetime is deliberately weaker. Add the validated reported expiry to the token result, or make an absolute notAfter bound part of the mint API and perform it before exposing/claiming the token. State the handoff metadata needed for job_tokens in the same API. The real Room deadline test must use this production API, reject a generic-valid token expiring after the job deadline, owe/revoke its ID and send no job. Include the boundary between wake delay, create delay, answer and dispatch.\n\n2. P2: R-MINT-6 still overstates unknown check-token exposure. docs/protocol.md:3357 says check job tokens end by the job deadline in the very paragraph describing a create with unknown outcome. Existing packages/room/test/workerd/job-token-mint.test.ts:123-177 explicitly holds a create until after that deadline, loses its answer and proves the token is live past the deadline; no caller has its text and its duty remains open. This is the documented unresolved provider effect, not an accepted job credential. Limit the absolute deadline guarantee to tokens accepted and issued to checkers; describe unknown late-applied creates using the requested lifetime and the adjacent apply-time assumption, without claiming their calendar expiry is bounded by the original deadline. Apply this distinction consistently in the sites table and out-of-scope lifetime text, and retain the late-apply/lost-answer control.\n\n3. P2: the overdue wake rule is impossible as written. R-MINT-7 requires a stored wake no later than each owed due time and also requires every stored wake to be in the future. A takeover makes held tokens owed due now; after a 20-record pass a larger ready backlog still has past due times. No timestamp satisfies both requirements. Define due time as eligibility and specify a bounded positive continuation delay for overdue/backlogged work, with the in-flight pass's timeout suppressing repeated immediate wakes. Future takeover/observation deadlines must still be stored on time and earlier unrelated alarms preserved. Add a real-DO control with more than one revocation batch already due, a held revoke and no further request: no immediate alarm spin, no queue blocking or starvation, and all batches remain reachable.\n\n4. P2: the new keyed-ID rule conflicts with permitted expiry settlement. R-MINT-7 at docs/protocol.md:3372 requires an ID-keyed record until a transaction marks it revoked, but R-MINT-4 at :3317 allows the owner to end once a readable provider expiry passes. job_tokens already removes a known expired duty after a revoke failure; the ledger's owed state likewise permits that end. Define the indexed record's lifetime through revocation confirmation or valid known-expiry settlement, matching its owning record; keep unknown creates separate. Confirm in future lane controls that an expired known token can settle without a fake revocation marker and an unreadable expiry cannot.\n\nIndependent evidence: read the complete 420-line revision 2 design, its full protocol delta and the complete revision 3 diff; checked actual Room wake/nextAlarm, jobs.issue and late-mint controls, and LandingCore storage/handoff/revocation sites. Read primary Cloudflare Workers binding and REST pages on 2026-10-02 (https://developers.cloudflare.com/artifacts/api/workers-binding/ and https://developers.cloudflare.com/artifacts/api/rest-api/). git diff --check at this docs-only head passes. No source implementation, runtime guards, live calls or executed future-lane tests are claimed.\n\nStaleness: main has since incorporated hygiene 7cf6aae0 and the join/redemption work, whose protocol also adds section 31. Reconcile section numbering and pointers when this design lands; no source line pointer is asserted to be current main. D5's retirement also moves the Git harness to measure/harness, so future lane requests should target the then-current path. These are landing concerns, not authority for unreviewed implementation.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c9ccb233e1bff9c2832656980ab260a4ea8fd3ce","binding":"assigned","head":"cfb8d0fe0db769a3404e7685fbbb3271596feaab","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c07f13b2925eacdad0c4c24c321c91a19a6a2b72\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a6e2c3de639d14f85772b4255a59954169024daa\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c07f13b2925eacdad0c4c24c321c91a19a6a2b72","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}